1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Of everything humans have ever made, the farthest is one small spacecraft

2
00:00:07,251 --> 00:00:08,556
Voyager 1

3
00:00:08,556 --> 00:00:13,936
It is so far from Earth that even light needs a whole day to reach it

4
00:00:13,936 --> 00:00:16,270
It has been travelling for 49 years

5
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:21,209
And all of its computers' memory is smaller than a single photo on today's phones

6
00:00:21,209 --> 00:00:26,933
Three years ago, this machine suddenly started sending meaningless signals

7
00:00:26,933 --> 00:00:29,440
The same pattern, over and over

8
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:34,682
To fix it, engineers had to reason with a machine from that far away

9
00:00:34,682 --> 00:00:41,328
A machine whose builders had mostly retired, and whose manuals were on paper

10
00:00:41,328 --> 00:00:46,395
The next five months were the longest the Voyager team had known

11
00:00:46,395 --> 00:00:50,585
But the biggest question of this story is still open

12
00:00:50,585 --> 00:00:54,289
How did this human-made thing get so far...

13
00:00:54,289 --> 00:00:59,088
...and when its voice falls silent forever, what will go with it?

14
00:01:06,388 --> 00:01:07,188
This story begins in 1965

15
00:01:07,162 --> 00:01:08,892
1965

16
00:01:08,892 --> 00:01:15,892
Until then, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, the four giant planets, were a mystery

17
00:01:16,745 --> 00:01:18,080
The problem was distance

18
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:21,923
Flying straight to Neptune would take about 30 years

19
00:01:21,923 --> 00:01:25,859
No machine, it seemed, could keep working that long

20
00:01:25,859 --> 00:01:32,859
Then Gary Flandro, a young student working at NASA's JPL, worked out the motions of the planets

21
00:01:33,313 --> 00:01:40,313
He saw that between 1976 and 1979, all four planets would line up in a special way

22
00:01:41,793 --> 00:01:46,622
A chance like that comes about once every 175 years

23
00:01:46,622 --> 00:01:49,681
And this line-up had an amazing advantage

24
00:01:49,681 --> 00:01:56,681
When a spacecraft passes a planet, the planet's gravity pulls it and flings it onward

25
00:01:56,908 --> 00:02:02,834
Just as a cricket ball speeds up off a swinging bat

26
00:02:02,834 --> 00:02:08,441
Planet to planet, each time a little more speed

27
00:02:08,441 --> 00:02:13,642
That cut the trip to Neptune from 30 years to just 12

28
00:02:13,642 --> 00:02:19,452
Had they missed it, the next chance would come around 2150

29
00:02:19,452 --> 00:02:21,744
NASA did not miss it

30
00:02:23,144 --> 00:02:26,006
There was one chance, so NASA built two spacecraft

31
00:02:26,006 --> 00:02:29,001
If one failed, the other would finish the job

32
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:32,472
They were named Voyager 1 and Voyager 2

33
00:02:32,472 --> 00:02:34,927
But one big question remained

34
00:02:34,927 --> 00:02:39,402
That far out, sunlight grows very weak

35
00:02:39,402 --> 00:02:44,325
At Jupiter it is about 27 times weaker than at Earth

36
00:02:44,325 --> 00:02:46,380
Solar panels can't work there

37
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:49,758
So inside each Voyager they put plutonium

38
00:02:49,758 --> 00:02:56,735
Plutonium keeps giving off heat, like an ember that stays hot for years

39
00:02:56,735 --> 00:02:59,480
And that heat makes electricity

40
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:02,777
That electricity keeps Voyager alive to this day

41
00:03:02,777 --> 00:03:05,987
And that same electricity is slowly running out

42
00:03:05,987 --> 00:03:10,027
In 1977, Voyager 2 flew first

43
00:03:10,027 --> 00:03:12,644
Two weeks later, Voyager 1

44
00:03:12,644 --> 00:03:18,658
Voyager 1 left later, but it was given a shorter, faster path to Jupiter

45
00:03:18,658 --> 00:03:21,960
Within months, it overtook Voyager 2

46
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:26,934
But besides their science gear, both carried one more thing

47
00:03:26,934 --> 00:03:30,039
A message, for someone unknown

48
00:03:31,439 --> 00:03:35,183
Voyager's paths meant that after the planets, they would never come back

49
00:03:35,183 --> 00:03:39,948
They would leave the solar system and wander among the stars forever

50
00:03:39,948 --> 00:03:46,948
So astronomer Carl Sagan and his team decided to send a message with them

51
00:03:47,441 --> 00:03:52,224
A gold-plated record, like the gramophone records of the old days

52
00:03:52,224 --> 00:03:54,546
This record holds pictures of Earth

53
00:03:54,546 --> 00:03:58,371
Sounds like wind, rain, and a heartbeat

54
00:03:58,371 --> 00:04:02,265
Music from around the world, about 90 minutes of it

55
00:04:02,265 --> 00:04:06,160
And greetings from Earth in 55 languages

56
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:08,789
One of them is in Hindi

57
00:04:08,789 --> 00:04:11,135
Greetings from the people of Earth

58
00:04:11,135 --> 00:04:13,439
The record also carries a song from India

59
00:04:13,439 --> 00:04:18,594
Sung by Surshri Kesarbai Kerkar: raag Bhairavi, "Jaat Kahan Ho

60
00:04:18,594 --> 00:04:21,043
Perhaps no one will ever find it

61
00:04:21,043 --> 00:04:24,578
But the record is a picture of who humans were

62
00:04:24,578 --> 00:04:29,245
In space there is no air, no water, no soil to wear it down

63
00:04:29,245 --> 00:04:34,249
So this picture may survive, unchanged, for a billion years

64
00:04:34,249 --> 00:04:40,778
But before this message reached anyone, Voyager had a real job to do

65
00:04:42,178 --> 00:04:48,511
Until 1979, scientists thought the moons of the giant planets were just cold, dead rock

66
00:04:48,511 --> 00:04:53,114
In March 1979, Voyager 1 reached Jupiter

67
00:04:53,114 --> 00:05:00,114
One day, NASA engineer Linda Morabito was looking at an image of Jupiter's moon Io

68
00:05:00,252 --> 00:05:04,339
Her job was to check Voyager's path using the stars

69
00:05:04,339 --> 00:05:08,291
Then she saw a strange cloud at the edge of Io

70
00:05:08,291 --> 00:05:11,826
It was a volcano, erupting at that moment

71
00:05:11,826 --> 00:05:16,331
The first active volcano ever found on any world beyond Earth

72
00:05:16,331 --> 00:05:17,857
Then came Saturn

73
00:05:17,857 --> 00:05:22,832
In 1980, Voyager 1 was sent close to Saturn's moon Titan

74
00:05:22,832 --> 00:05:28,800
Titan has a thick blanket of air, and scientists wanted a close look

75
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:31,708
But that decision had a price

76
00:05:31,708 --> 00:05:38,708
All the planets circle the Sun on nearly the same flat path, like marbles rolling on a plate

77
00:05:39,829 --> 00:05:43,370
Titan's gravity did what the planets had done before

78
00:05:43,370 --> 00:05:46,881
It bent Voyager 1's path up, out of the plate

79
00:05:46,881 --> 00:05:49,974
Now it could never reach another planet

80
00:05:49,974 --> 00:05:52,842
Voyager 2 made the rest of the journey alone

81
00:05:52,842 --> 00:05:58,013
Uranus in 1986, and Neptune in 1989

82
00:05:58,013 --> 00:06:04,555
To this day, Voyager 2 is the only spacecraft to have visited these two planets

83
00:06:04,555 --> 00:06:06,703
The planets were done

84
00:06:06,703 --> 00:06:12,953
But before Voyager 1's cameras switched off, it had one last picture to take

85
00:06:14,353 --> 00:06:19,031
By 1990, Voyager 1 was about 6 billion kilometres from Earth

86
00:06:19,031 --> 00:06:23,710
To save power, NASA was about to switch its cameras off for the last time

87
00:06:23,710 --> 00:06:27,268
Then Carl Sagan made an odd demand

88
00:06:27,268 --> 00:06:29,741
Turn the camera around, toward Earth

89
00:06:29,741 --> 00:06:31,714
Many were against it

90
00:06:31,714 --> 00:06:36,056
A picture of Earth from that far was of no use to science

91
00:06:36,056 --> 00:06:40,410
In the end Sagan won, and Voyager looked back

92
00:06:40,410 --> 00:06:46,296
In that picture, inside a ray of sunlight, is a tiny blue dot

93
00:06:46,296 --> 00:06:48,217
Smaller than a single pixel

94
00:06:48,217 --> 00:06:49,436
That is our Earth

95
00:06:49,436 --> 00:06:55,183
Every person, every city, every war ever fought, all on that one dot

96
00:06:55,183 --> 00:07:00,157
Soon after, Voyager's cameras were switched off, never to open again

97
00:07:00,157 --> 00:07:02,386
These were its last pictures

98
00:07:02,386 --> 00:07:06,400
But Voyager's longest journey had only just begun

99
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,036
The Sun doesn't only give light

100
00:07:09,036 --> 00:07:14,249
Every moment it blows a wind of tiny particles out in all directions

101
00:07:14,249 --> 00:07:16,576
It's called the solar wind

102
00:07:16,576 --> 00:07:22,137
This wind wraps all the planets in a huge bubble

103
00:07:22,137 --> 00:07:26,014
Where the wind stops, the bubble ends

104
00:07:26,014 --> 00:07:28,940
Beyond it begins the space between the stars

105
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:30,838
Interstellar space

106
00:07:30,838 --> 00:07:33,206
Scientists had a problem

107
00:07:33,206 --> 00:07:35,668
There's no sign at the edge

108
00:07:35,668 --> 00:07:37,850
And Voyager's cameras were off

109
00:07:37,850 --> 00:07:42,557
They needed a sign that Voyager itself could feel

110
00:07:42,557 --> 00:07:44,629
The answer came from the Sun itself

111
00:07:44,629 --> 00:07:48,686
In 2012, there was a huge blast on the Sun

112
00:07:48,686 --> 00:07:55,674
When its wave reached Voyager, the gas around Voyager rang like a bell

113
00:07:55,674 --> 00:07:58,310
One of Voyager's instruments heard that ringing

114
00:07:58,310 --> 00:08:02,652
Tapping a pot tells you whether it is empty or full

115
00:08:02,652 --> 00:08:07,981
In the same way, the ringing told them how dense the gas was

116
00:08:07,981 --> 00:08:13,577
It was about 40 times denser than the gas inside the Sun's bubble

117
00:08:13,577 --> 00:08:16,427
So it wasn't the Sun's gas at all

118
00:08:16,427 --> 00:08:18,627
It was the gas between the stars

119
00:08:18,627 --> 00:08:25,627
In 2013 NASA announced that back in 2012, Voyager 1 had become the first human-made object in interstellar space

120
00:08:28,710 --> 00:08:35,613
Voyager's project manager, Suzanne Dodd, later explained what Voyager is doing out there

121
00:08:35,613 --> 00:08:42,613
Suzanne Dodd, Voyager project manager: "Voyager is really like a weather satellite traveling through interstellar space. And what interstellar space is contained of, it's dust from other stars. So what Voyager is measuring is the density of the dust and plasma that it's traveling through.

122
00:08:51,538 --> 00:08:55,397
In 2018, Voyager 2 got there too

123
00:08:56,797 --> 00:09:03,797
Now back to three years ago, to the day Voyager began sending meaningless signals

124
00:09:03,878 --> 00:09:06,189
Talking to Voyager is not easy

125
00:09:06,189 --> 00:09:11,553
Its radio is just 22 watts, about as strong as a fridge light bulb

126
00:09:11,553 --> 00:09:18,553
By the time its voice reaches Earth it's so faint that it takes a 70-metre dish to hear it

127
00:09:19,819 --> 00:09:24,735
And every message takes about two days to go and come back

128
00:09:24,735 --> 00:09:31,735
Working like this, engineers found that a chip in its 1977 computer had failed

129
00:09:32,031 --> 00:09:36,267
About 3 percent of its memory was ruined

130
00:09:36,267 --> 00:09:38,241
The chip could not be fixed

131
00:09:38,241 --> 00:09:42,171
So the team sent Voyager a special command

132
00:09:42,171 --> 00:09:46,455
In reply, Voyager sent back a copy of its entire memory

133
00:09:46,455 --> 00:09:52,172
From that copy, the team saw which part of the program was stuck in the broken spot

134
00:09:52,172 --> 00:09:57,181
Then they did what you'd do when one room of a house breaks

135
00:09:57,181 --> 00:10:01,651
They moved its things, bit by bit, into the other rooms

136
00:10:01,651 --> 00:10:07,217
That part of the program was moved, in pieces, to good places in the memory

137
00:10:07,217 --> 00:10:10,461
In April 2024, the fix was sent

138
00:10:10,461 --> 00:10:12,923
Two days later, Voyager answered

139
00:10:12,923 --> 00:10:15,726
Clear this time, and making sense

140
00:10:15,726 --> 00:10:19,232
After five months, Voyager 1 was talking again

141
00:10:19,232 --> 00:10:24,987
And when that answer arrived, this is how the team in a room at JPL took it

142
00:10:24,987 --> 00:10:31,987
Voyager team, NASA JPL: "Oh, yeah. Oh, wow. That's insane. There we go. That's insane. All right!

143
00:10:35,658 --> 00:10:38,880
But Voyager's biggest problem isn't a chip

144
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:45,880
The plutonium put in for power in 1977 is slowly running down

145
00:10:46,020 --> 00:10:49,798
Its power drops by about 4 watts every year

146
00:10:49,798 --> 00:10:54,907
By 2026, Voyager has less than half of the power it started with

147
00:10:54,907 --> 00:10:59,237
So the team is switching its instruments off, one by one

148
00:10:59,237 --> 00:11:03,764
In April 2026, one more instrument was switched off

149
00:11:03,764 --> 00:11:08,292
Now Voyager 1 has just two science instruments left

150
00:11:08,292 --> 00:11:11,177
On this long journey, its people changed too

151
00:11:11,177 --> 00:11:18,119
Ed Stone, Voyager's chief scientist for 50 years, passed away in 2024

152
00:11:18,119 --> 00:11:20,562
And now the day is coming

153
00:11:20,562 --> 00:11:24,823
Light from the Moon reaches Earth in 1.3 seconds

154
00:11:24,823 --> 00:11:27,285
From the Sun, in about 8 minutes

155
00:11:27,285 --> 00:11:33,529
And from 18 November 2026, from Voyager 1: one whole day

156
00:11:33,529 --> 00:11:35,275
One light-day

157
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:40,918
One day, perhaps within a few years, Voyager's last word will arrive

158
00:11:40,918 --> 00:11:45,527
After that, NASA's dishes will keep listening, but no answer will come

159
00:11:45,527 --> 00:11:47,965
But Voyager won't stop

160
00:11:47,965 --> 00:11:52,633
It will keep going at about 61,000 kilometres an hour

161
00:11:52,633 --> 00:11:57,468
Fast enough to fly all the way around Earth in just 40 minutes

162
00:11:57,468 --> 00:12:04,468
In about 40,000 years, it will pass a star called Gliese 445

163
00:12:04,718 --> 00:12:10,912
Like a light-day, a light-year is the distance light travels in a year

164
00:12:10,912 --> 00:12:16,056
And even then, Voyager will pass 1.6 light-years from that star

165
00:12:16,056 --> 00:12:19,608
But it carries a golden record

166
00:12:19,608 --> 00:12:25,547
Holding a heartbeat, Kesarbai's voice, and a message in Hindi

167
00:12:25,547 --> 00:12:28,345
Greetings from the people of Earth

168
00:12:28,345 --> 00:12:35,345
The machine that once sent meaningless signals has set out forever, carrying humanity's longest message

169
00:12:37,963 --> 00:12:41,057
Its voice will fall silent one day

170
00:12:41,057 --> 00:12:45,293
But our message will keep going

